The escapeSqlStringValue method doesn't handle control characters. While SQL is more lenient than JSON, certain control characters in string literals might cause issues depending on the SQL client or logging. Consider whether control character escaping is needed for MariaDB string literals.
```suggestion
	 * unintended escape sequences. Common control characters are escaped using MariaDB
	 * backslash escape sequences to avoid issues in clients and logs.
	 * @param input the string to escape
	 * @return the escaped string safe for use in SQL string literals
	 * @author Zexuan Peng &lt;pengzexuan2001@gmail.com&gt;
	 */
	private String escapeSqlStringValue(String input) {
		StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder(input.length() + 16);
		for (int i = 0; i < input.length(); i++) {
			char c = input.charAt(i);
			switch (c) {
				case '\\' -> sb.append("\\\\");
				case '\'' -> sb.append("''");
				case '\0' -> sb.append("\\0");
				case '\b' -> sb.append("\\b");
				case '\n' -> sb.append("\\n");
				case '\r' -> sb.append("\\r");
				case '\t' -> sb.append("\\t");
				case 26 -> sb.append("\\Z"); // Ctrl+Z / SUB
				default -> {
					if (c < 0x20) {
						String hex = Integer.toHexString(c);
						if (hex.length() == 1) {
							hex = "0" + hex;
						}
						sb.append("\\x").append(hex);
					}
					else {
						sb.append(c);
					}
				}
			}
		}
		return sb.toString();
```

